@page "/guides/testing"

<PageTitle>Testing · Trailblazor</PageTitle>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Headline" As="TextElement.H1">Testing</TbText>
<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" class="docs-lead">
    Pages built with Trailblazor are meant to be easy to test. One small library sets up bUnit, another drives
    components in Playwright, and every component can be found by its role and name, the way users find it.
</TbText>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">bUnit</TbText>
<TbText>
    Reference <code>Trailblazor.Testing</code> and call <code>this.AddTrailblazor()</code> in the test class, before
    anything renders. It registers Trailblazor's services and answers its JavaScript calls, so components render and
    respond to clicks with no JSInterop setup. A call you set up yourself still wins. The browser service is a
    <code>FakeBrowserService</code> that records what was copied, downloaded and saved; the
    <a href="guides/browser">browser guide</a> shows it.
</TbText>
<TbText>
    Components then render as they do once a page is interactive, with no renderer setup: the first component a test
    renders makes bUnit's renderer an interactive one. To test what a statically rendered or prerendered page shows,
    which differs for a few components, such as a select that renders a native <code>&lt;select&gt;</code>, call
    <code>SetRendererInfo(new RendererInfo("Static", isInteractive: false))</code> before rendering; each
    component's page says how it differs. Register your own services, such as a <code>FakeTimeProvider</code>,
    first: bUnit takes none once a component renders, a service is resolved or <code>SetRendererInfo</code> is
    called.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="A bUnit test" Code="@Bunit" />
<TbText>
    To drive fields the way a user does, <code>cut.Tb()</code> finds them by their labels, with the same names as the
    Playwright helpers below: choose an option in a select or list box by its text, read what a select shows, type a
    number, and pick a tab. As in Playwright, choosing keeps options already chosen and refuses a disabled field.
    Choosing needs an interactive page, since a static select is a native one that posts with its form. A number field
    without a <code>Format</code> reads what was typed through its own change event, which bUnit's
    <code>Change</code> doesn't reach; <code>EnterAsync</code> types into either kind. A field's error is also announced
    in <code>TbRoot</code>'s live region, so a check that the markup no longer holds an error looks at the field, not
    the whole page.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Driving fields in bUnit" Code="@BunitDrivers" />
<TbText>
    To test a dialog by itself, <code>this.ShowDialogAsync&lt;TDialog, TResult&gt;()</code> opens it as a page would:
    it renders a <code>TbRoot</code> to host it and opens it through
    <code>IDialogService</code> with the title, parameters and options you give. Find what it shows through
    <code>Root</code>, its component through <code>Content</code>, and close it or await its result through
    <code>Reference</code>. A second call in the same test opens its dialog in the same <code>TbRoot</code>, so
    don't render one of your own as well.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Testing a dialog in bUnit" Code="@BunitDialog" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Playwright</TbText>
<TbText>
    Reference <code>Trailblazor.Testing.Playwright</code> for helpers that drive components the way a person does. A
    select is chosen by its label and option names, whether it renders as Trailblazor's list or, on a static page, as a
    native <code>&lt;select&gt;</code>. A popover opens by its trigger's name and hands back its panel. The helpers
    wait for the page to be ready.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="A Playwright test" Code="@Playwright" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Waiting for the page</TbText>
<TbText>
    A page shows before its event handlers are attached, so a click that comes too soon does nothing. Playwright's
    <code>Expect</code> assertions retry, but clicks and key presses don't, so wait first:
</TbText>
<ul class="docs-notes">
    <li>
        For the page, when <code>Routes</code> is interactive, mark your layout once it is, as with
        <code>&lt;main data-interactive="@@RendererInfo.IsInteractive"&gt;</code>, and wait for
        <code>main[data-interactive]</code> after each navigation. When interactivity is set page by page, the layout
        stays static, so put the marker on each interactive page's root instead.
    </li>
    <li>
        For a component whose keys or pointer gestures need its script, wait for <code>data-ready</code> on it where
        its page says so, such as a tree, a data grid, a splitter or an accordion.
    </li>
    <li>A menu's trigger gets <code>aria-expanded</code> once its menu can open.</li>
    <li>
        A data grid with a <code>DataProvider</code> sets <code>aria-busy</code> on its table while rows load; read the
        rows once it clears.
    </li>
</ul>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Time and motion</TbText>
<TbText>
    Toasts, announcements, a text field's <code>UpdateDelay</code> and an autocomplete's
    <code>DebounceDelay</code> run on timers. In bUnit, register a <code>FakeTimeProvider</code> as the
    <code>TimeProvider</code> before <code>AddTrailblazor()</code> and advance it, rather than waiting. A tooltip's
    delays run in the browser instead, so set its <code>ShowDelay</code> to zero or use Playwright's
    <code>page.Clock</code>. In Playwright, open pages with <code>ReducedMotion = ReducedMotion.Reduce</code>, and
    transitions finish at once.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Time and motion" Code="@TimeAndMotion" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Finding components</TbText>
<TbText>
    Find components by role and accessible name rather than by CSS classes, which can change between versions. Buttons
    are buttons and text fields are found by their labels. A select or autocomplete is a <code>combobox</code> with
    <code>option</code>s; find it with <code>GetByRole(AriaRole.Combobox, new() { Name = "Size" })</code>, since its
    label names its list of options too. A menu is a <code>menu</code> of <code>menuitem</code>s, a dialog is a
    <code>dialog</code> named by its title, a popover's panel is a <code>dialog</code> named by its title or trigger,
    and tabs are <code>tab</code>s with <code>tabpanel</code>s.
</TbText>
<TbText>
    Every component also marks its parts with a stable <code>data-part</code> attribute, for the parts that have no
    role of their own, such as a field's <code>[data-part=helper]</code> text or <code>[data-part=error]</code>
    message. Each component's page lists its parts. What is read out goes into <code>TbRoot</code>'s
    <code>[data-part=announcer]</code>, or a modal dialog's own, as a <code>p</code> in
    <code>[data-part=polite]</code> or <code>[data-part=assertive]</code>; several can be there at once, so find
    the one with your text.
</TbText>
<TbText>
    An <code>id</code> you give a field names its control, as on a native input: <code>&lt;TbTextField
    id="email" /&gt;</code> puts <code>id="email"</code> on the input, and its label points to it, so
    <code>#email</code> reaches what the user types into. A checkbox or switch gives it to its box and a select to its
    combobox. A field with two controls, a date range picker or range slider, and a checkbox or radio group keep the id
    on their root.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Finding components" Code="@Locators" />

@code {
    private const string Bunit = """
        using Trailblazor.Testing;

        public class SettingsPageTests : BunitContext
        {
            public SettingsPageTests() => this.AddTrailblazor();

            [Fact]
            public void Save_ShouldShowAToast_WhenClicked()
            {
                var page = Render<SettingsPage>();

                page.Find("button").Click();

                Assert.Single(Services.GetRequiredService<IToastService>().Toasts);
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string BunitDrivers = """
        var page = Render<OrderPage>();

        await page.Tb().Select("Size").ChooseAsync("Medium");
        await page.Tb().Select("Toppings").ChooseAsync("Olives", "Basil");
        await page.Tb().ListBox("Camp").ChooseAsync("Meadow");
        await page.Tb().NumberField("Copies").EnterAsync("12");
        await page.Tb().Tabs().SelectAsync("Route");

        Assert.Equal("Olives, Basil", page.Tb().Select("Toppings").Text);
        """;

    private const string BunitDialog = """
        [Fact]
        public async Task Save_ShouldCloseWithTheNewName_WhenClicked()
        {
            this.AddTrailblazor();
            var dialog = await this.ShowDialogAsync<RenameDialog, string>("Rename trail",
                parameters => parameters.Set(rename => rename.Name, "Pine Hollow"));

            await dialog.Root.Find("input").InputAsync(new() { Value = "Eagle Ridge" });
            await dialog.Root.FindAll("button").Single(button => button.TextContent.Trim() == "Save").ClickAsync(new());

            Assert.Equal("Eagle Ridge", (await dialog.Reference.Result).Value);
        }
        """;

    private const string Playwright = """
        using Trailblazor.Testing.Playwright;

        await page.Tb().Select("Size").ChooseAsync("Medium");
        await page.Tb().Select("Notify me about").ChooseAsync("Orders", "Returns");
        await page.Tb().ListBox("Gear to pack").ChooseAsync("Tent", "Stove");
        await page.Tb().ColorPicker("Trail marker").ChooseAsync("#d1242f");
        await page.Tb().FileUpload("Trail maps").ChooseAsync("map.pdf");
        await page.Tb().Tree("Trails by region").SelectAsync("River valley", "Fern Gully", "Waterfall spur");
        await page.Tb().DateRangePicker("Trip dates").ChooseAsync(new DateOnly(2026, 7, 2), new DateOnly(2026, 7, 9));

        var filters = await page.Tb().Popover("Filters").OpenAsync();
        await filters.GetByLabel("Only open items").CheckAsync();
        await page.Tb().Popover("Filters").CloseAsync();
        """;

    private const string TimeAndMotion = """
        // bUnit: toasts close when the fake time passes their duration.
        var time = new FakeTimeProvider();
        Services.AddSingleton<TimeProvider>(time);
        this.AddTrailblazor();
        time.Advance(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

        // Playwright: a page with no transitions.
        var page = await browser.NewPageAsync(new() { ReducedMotion = ReducedMotion.Reduce });
        """;

    private const string Locators = """
        await page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Save" }).ClickAsync();
        await page.GetByLabel("Name").FillAsync("Ada");
        await page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Combobox, new() { Name = "Size" }).ClickAsync();
        await page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Dialog, new() { Name = "Rename" })
            .GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Close" }).ClickAsync();
        await Expect(page.Locator("[data-part=polite] p").Filter(new() { HasText = "Saved." }).Last)
            .ToBeAttachedAsync();
        """;
}
